9 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "22420a9a-1575-5698-aa34-04729efa2a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483254 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "346d1a8e-e297-5ff1-8dff-7d5740531d20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483341 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb66c9a3-57be-589b-8044-dbc471e3b934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483360 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483360 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483360 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483360 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483360 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3aad6c37-ff96-5a6c-89b9-a9f32a30b3d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483360 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483377 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{3}y\\,j+z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75ca7d55-6e3f-58c8-8a50-805ce5d1a756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483377 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483395 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483395 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483395 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483395 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c1/c1dd3e659d2580103232fa68620c60f493477649e1c4a8880023e6c6de88a167.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483395 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aefd685f-6837-5195-a54c-56ec619b88d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483395 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components decrease as we move vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483433 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483433 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05100288-b9ed-59fd-b7a9-9a2592fbbb50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483433 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483443 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b54e950-07e6-508f-8bbf-26f7181e85f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483443 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186266056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483450 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483450 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e0d18ce-acf0-5440-b4c0-fe1c45eb2401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483450 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186259490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483483 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483483 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e3f811c-54a6-5c26-bdfa-8aaa897c63e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483483 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483488 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186483483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "adea8609-2822-5adc-b454-084b01390c94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186483377 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
